#: src/environment.md:14
msgid ""
"To make the best use of these features, it helps to understand where Zed "
"gets environment variables and how it uses them."
msgstr ""

#: src/environment.md:16
msgid "Where does Zed get its environment variables from?"
msgstr ""

#: src/environment.md:18
msgid ""
"How Zed starts affects which environment variables it can use. That includes "
"launching from the macOS Dock, a Linux window manager, or the `zed` CLI."
msgstr ""

#: src/environment.md:20
msgid "Launched from the CLI"
msgstr ""

#: src/environment.md:22
msgid ""
"If Zed is opened via the CLI (`zed`), it will inherit the environment "
"variables from the surrounding shell session."
msgstr ""

#: src/environment.md:24
msgid "That means if you do"
msgstr ""

#: src/environment.md:31
msgid ""
"the environment variable `MY_ENV_VAR` is now available inside Zed. For "
"example, in the built-in terminal."
msgstr ""

#: src/environment.md:33
msgid ""
"Starting with Zed 0.152.0, the CLI `zed` will _always_ pass along its "
"environment to Zed, regardless of whether a Zed instance was previously "
"running or not. Prior to Zed 0.152.0 this was not the case and only the "
"first Zed instance would inherit the environment variables."
msgstr ""

#: src/environment.md:35
msgid "Launched via window manager, Dock, or launcher"
msgstr ""

#: src/environment.md:37
msgid ""
"When Zed has been launched via the macOS Dock, or a GNOME or KDE icon on "
"Linux, or an application launcher like Alfred or Raycast, it has no "
"surrounding shell environment from which to inherit its environment "
"variables."
msgstr ""

#: src/environment.md:39
msgid ""
"To still have a useful environment, Zed spawns a login shell in the user's "
"home directory and reads its environment. This environment is then set on "
"the Zed _process_, so all Zed windows and projects inherit it."
msgstr ""

#: src/environment.md:41
msgid ""
"Since that can lead to problems for users who need different environment "
"variables per project (for example with `direnv`, `asdf`, or `mise`), Zed "
"spawns another login shell when opening a project. This second shell runs in "
"the project's directory. The environment from that shell is _not_ set on the "
"process, because opening a new project would otherwise change the "
"environment for all Zed windows. Instead, that environment is stored and "
"passed along when running tasks, opening terminals, or spawning language "
"servers."
msgstr ""

#: src/environment.md:43
msgid "Where and how are environment variables used?"
msgstr ""

#: src/environment.md:45
msgid "There are two sets of environment variables:"
msgstr ""

#: src/environment.md:47
msgid "Environment variables of the Zed process"
msgstr ""

#: src/environment.md:48
msgid "Environment variables stored per project"
msgstr ""

#: src/environment.md:50
msgid ""
"The variables from (1) are always used, since they are stored on the process "
"itself and every spawned process (tasks, terminals, language servers, ...) "
"will inherit them by default."
msgstr ""

#: src/environment.md:52
msgid "The variables from (2) are used explicitly, depending on the feature."
msgstr ""

#: src/environment.md:54
msgid "Tasks"
msgstr ""

#: src/environment.md:56
msgid ""
"Tasks are spawned with a combined environment. In order of precedence (low "
"to high, with the last overwriting the first):"
msgstr ""

#: src/environment.md:58 src/environment.md:67
msgid "the Zed process environment"
msgstr ""

#: src/environment.md:59 src/environment.md:68 src/environment.md:84
msgid "if the project was opened from the CLI: the CLI environment"
msgstr ""

#: src/environment.md:60 src/environment.md:69 src/environment.md:85
msgid ""
"if the project was not opened from the CLI: the project environment "
"variables obtained by running a login shell in the project's root folder"
msgstr ""

#: src/environment.md:61 src/environment.md:70
msgid "optional, explicitly configured environment in settings"
msgstr ""

#: src/environment.md:63
msgid "Built-in terminal"
msgstr ""

#: src/environment.md:65
msgid ""
"Built-in terminals, like tasks, are spawned with a combined environment. In "
"order of precedence (low to high):"
msgstr ""

